WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.302
A payment service stores each card authorization as a key and a status.

2
00:00:07.302 --> 00:00:12.164
The cheapest way to write is to append every change to the end of one file.

3
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:16.850
When auth:9001 is captured, its new record goes at the end too.

4
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.337
So which record should a read of auth:9001 trust?

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:25.255
With only the file, you scan it from the start and keep the last match you see.

6
00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:31.212
With a million records, every lookup reads 40 megabytes, and it gets slower with each write.

7
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:37.409
Bitcask, the engine built for the Riak database, adds a hash map in memory called the keydir.

8
00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:41.739
For each key, it holds the byte offset of that key's newest record.

9
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:48.569
A get is one lookup in the map, then one read of exactly those bytes: 37 bytes at offset 75.

10
00:00:48.569 --> 00:00:52.464
A key that isn't in the map costs no disk read at all.

11
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:56.385
A put appends the record, then moves that key's entry to it.

12
00:00:56.385 --> 00:00:59.421
The old record is dead now: nothing points at it.

13
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:02.797
Every update leaves one more dead record behind.

14
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:13.780
In the lab, ten card balances updated a hundred times each leave a thousand records: 36 kilobytes of file for 370 bytes of live data.

15
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:15.990
So the log is cut into segments.

16
00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:23.452
When the active segment reaches its size limit, it's closed and never changes again, and new writes go to a fresh one.

17
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:28.065
Then auth:9003 is authorized and auth:9001 is refunded.

18
00:01:28.065 --> 00:01:31.424
Their older records in segment 1 are dead.

19
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.209
A merge runs in the background over the closed segments.

20
00:01:35.209 --> 00:01:39.720
It copies only the records the keydir still points to into a new file.

21
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:44.362
The keydir switches to the new copy, and segment 1 is deleted.

22
00:01:44.362 --> 00:01:46.005
Six records became one.

23
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:53.673
A log store appends every write, keeps each key's newest offset in memory, and merges the dead records away later.
